 # Tetrahedral representation and traversal
-Also called: Projected tetrahedra family, tetrahedral cell projection
+Also called: Cell projection using tetrahedra, Projected Tetrahedra family
 
 ## Overview
-This family encompasses methods that decompose volumetric data into tetrahedral primitives to resolve topological ambiguities and irregularities inherent in hexahedral or cubic grids, then render them via projection, traversal, or analytic extraction. It solves the problem of rendering unstructured or curvilinear data efficiently by leveraging consistent tetrahedral topology, which ensures planar faces and single-intersection ray paths [S1, S2, S3, S4, book][S1, S8, S13, S19, unofficial][S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Marching Cubes](link), which suffers from ambiguous topologies at cube faces, tetrahedral decomposition provides a unique, unambiguous solution for iso-surface extraction and volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md) in that the algorithm is a specific iso-surface extraction technique, whereas this family includes broader rendering strategies like projected rasterization and parallel partitioning.
+This family covers methods that decompose volumetric data into tetrahedral primitives, project them onto the 2D image plane, and rasterize the resulting polygons to accumulate color and opacity [S2, unofficial][S3][S7]. It solves the problem of rendering unstructured or irregular grids (e.g., CFD data) efficiently by leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization instead of explicit per-pixel ray marching [S3][S5]. Unlike [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md), which extracts isosurfaces, this family renders the volume directly via translucent polygons [S11, unofficial]. It differs from [Tetrahedral Strips and Fans](1389-tetrahedral-strips-and-fans.md) by focusing on the general classification and decomposition of arbitrary tetrahedra rather than optimized connectivity [S13, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
 The cell projection family shares the following mechanism, terms, and math:
 
-1.  **Primitive Representation:** The volume is decomposed into convex polyhedral cells (e.g., tetrahedra, hexahedra). For regular grids (cubes or hexahedra), cells are often subdivided into tetrahedra (typically 5 or 6) to ensure planar faces and consistent topology [S2, S8, S13, S19, unofficial][S1, unofficial]. This decomposition resolves "problem cells" where rays might intersect a face twice in curvilinear grids [S4, book].
-2.  **Projection and Subdivision:** The 3D vertices of each cell are projected to 2D screen space. The projected edges of the cell divide the image plane into polygons, where each polygon lies within the projection of a single front-facing face and a single back-facing face of the cell [S1, unofficial][S2][S4, book]. For general cells, an incremental method builds this subdivision by adding projected edges one by one [S1, unofficial][S2]. For curvilinear grids, standard topologies are identified to use lookup tables for subdivision [S1, unofficial][S2].
-3.  **Scalar Interpolation:** Scalar values $s$ at any point within a tetrahedron are interpolated linearly from the vertex values $s_i$ using barycentric coordinates or linear interpolation along edges [S1, unofficial][S2][S3]. The scalar field is assumed to vary linearly across the cell, and transfer functions mapping scalar to color/extinction are assumed linear [S1, unofficial][S2][S4, book].
